1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,245
This phone was never
told which du'a this is.

2
00:00:07,245 --> 00:00:13,757
It worked it out from the words, out of
522 texts, and now it follows the recitation,

3
00:00:13,757 --> 00:00:16,425
line by line, word by word.

4
00:00:19,767 --> 00:00:22,285
You've probably used Shazam.

5
00:00:22,285 --> 00:00:27,656
Hold your phone up to a song in a cafe, and
a few seconds later it tells you its name.

6
00:00:27,656 --> 00:00:32,122
It turns those seconds of sound into
a picture of frequencies over time,

7
00:00:32,122 --> 00:00:34,855
and keeps only the brightest points.

8
00:00:34,855 --> 00:00:39,752
Pairs of points become short codes,
looked up among millions of songs.

9
00:00:39,752 --> 00:00:43,474
The song whose codes all line
up at the same moment wins.

10
00:00:43,483 --> 00:00:46,331
But those points belong to one recording.

11
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:50,084
Another reciter, another room,
and they change completely.

12
00:00:50,084 --> 00:00:53,128
What every recitation shares is the words.

13
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:57,749
Tarteel is an app for reciting the
Quran, and it listens for the words.

14
00:00:57,749 --> 00:01:01,420
You recite; speech recognition
writes down what you said;

15
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:07,088
that text is searched across the Quran to find
the surah and the ayah; and from then on,

16
00:01:07,088 --> 00:01:09,777
each word lights up as you say it.

17
00:01:09,777 --> 00:01:13,379
Hide the page, and the words
appear only as you recite them,

18
00:01:13,379 --> 00:01:16,019
and it can mark a word you missed.

19
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:21,885
Its founders started with an earlier app
that used a phone's own speech recognition.

20
00:01:21,885 --> 00:01:24,036
That was good enough to find an ayah,

21
00:01:24,036 --> 00:01:28,371
but it made more mistakes than the
reciters it was meant to check.

22
00:01:28,371 --> 00:01:34,192
So they crowdsourced recordings, curated
more than 75,000 minutes of recitation,

23
00:01:34,192 --> 00:01:37,020
and trained models of their own.

24
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:42,109
They published small ones too, and one
of those is where our phone model began.

25
00:01:42,117 --> 00:01:46,358
From Shazam: search everything at
once, from anywhere in a recording,

26
00:01:46,358 --> 00:01:49,563
and trust only matches that line up in time.

27
00:01:49,563 --> 00:01:53,133
From Tarteel: listen for
the words, and follow them.

28
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:58,023
Du'as add their own problems: many texts
share the same phrases, and people pause,

29
00:01:58,023 --> 00:02:00,472
go back and talk between lines.

30
00:02:00,483 --> 00:02:02,859
The data comes in three kinds.

31
00:02:02,859 --> 00:02:10,716
The texts: 522 du'as, ziyarat and
munajat, about 140,000 words.

32
00:02:10,716 --> 00:02:14,101
Recitations: DuaPlayer's are
timed line by line by hand,

33
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:17,904
so they are the ruler
everything is measured against.

34
00:02:17,917 --> 00:02:22,205
For more voices, we searched
YouTube, Shiavoice, Aparat,

35
00:02:22,205 --> 00:02:27,607
SoundCloud and the Internet Archive
for every text: 5,800 hours.

36
00:02:27,607 --> 00:02:33,758
A large letter model listened to every file,
found which text and which line it was,

37
00:02:33,758 --> 00:02:39,851
and kept only the lines that fit: about
1,400 hours, in about 3,500 voices.

38
00:02:39,867 --> 00:02:45,226
And ordinary voices, because reciters
aren't the people holding the phone:

39
00:02:45,226 --> 00:02:48,558
crowd-sourced recitations,
and synthetic ones,

40
00:02:48,558 --> 00:02:53,549
a voice-cloning model reading
du'as in 312 volunteers' voices.

41
00:02:53,549 --> 00:02:56,299
Test voices were removed
by name and by sound,

42
00:02:56,299 --> 00:03:00,008
so the results are on voices
the models never heard.

43
00:03:00,767 --> 00:03:03,796
Two models listen at
once, each with one job.

44
00:03:03,796 --> 00:03:06,321
Both read the same picture of sound.

45
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:09,838
The first is Whisper,
OpenAI's speech recogniser.

46
00:03:09,838 --> 00:03:12,250
One half listens, the other writes.

47
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:15,827
It reads the last 6 seconds
and writes down the words.

48
00:03:15,827 --> 00:03:22,857
On the phone it's Whisper-base, 74 million
parameters, grown from Tarteel's Quran model;

49
00:03:22,857 --> 00:03:26,892
on the server, large-v3-turbo, 809 million.

50
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:28,867
The second is a letter model.

51
00:03:28,867 --> 00:03:34,630
Fifty times a second, it answers one question:
which letter is being said now, or none.

52
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:39,911
A drawn-out word is one letter held over
many frames; a pause is all blanks.

53
00:03:39,911 --> 00:03:44,310
It's Whisper-base's listening half,
with one layer onto the alphabet:

54
00:03:44,310 --> 00:03:46,312
20 million parameters.

55
00:03:46,317 --> 00:03:50,965
Whisper was fine-tuned on du'a windows
labelled with the du'a's own text,

56
00:03:50,965 --> 00:03:53,376
never a machine's guess at it.

57
00:03:53,376 --> 00:03:59,073
Every window was altered on the way in: faster,
slower, a deeper or higher voice, noise,

58
00:03:59,073 --> 00:04:03,282
an echoing room, a hall through
loudspeakers with people talking,

59
00:04:03,282 --> 00:04:06,148
stripes of the picture blanked out.

60
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:11,873
Then each small model learnt from a big
one: the phone's Whisper from the server's,

61
00:04:11,873 --> 00:04:15,528
hearing a noisy copy of what
the big one hears clean;

62
00:04:15,528 --> 00:04:19,920
the letter model from a
300-million-parameter wav2vec2.

63
00:04:20,567 --> 00:04:22,412
Now, recognition.

64
00:04:22,412 --> 00:04:26,504
Every second or two, Whisper
writes down the last 6 seconds.

65
00:04:26,504 --> 00:04:28,192
Here, it hears this.

66
00:04:35,667 --> 00:04:40,534
The vowel marks and the spaces are
dropped, leaving a string of letters.

67
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:44,450
That string is slid along
every word of all 522 texts:

68
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:49,501
how many letters would have to
change for it to end right here?

69
00:04:49,501 --> 00:04:52,886
A trick by Gene Myers works
on 64 letters at once,

70
00:04:52,886 --> 00:04:56,736
so the whole library takes
about 3 milliseconds.

71
00:04:57,383 --> 00:04:59,508
One window isn't enough.

72
00:04:59,508 --> 00:05:00,738
Allāhumma

73
00:05:00,738 --> 00:05:03,635
is in 412 of the texts,

74
00:05:03,635 --> 00:05:05,545
Allāhumma innī asʾaluka

75
00:05:05,545 --> 00:05:07,265
in 106.

76
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:10,803
And since the matching
forgives a few wrong letters,

77
00:05:10,803 --> 00:05:13,389
for a second Du'a Jawshan Kabir leads:

78
00:05:13,389 --> 00:05:17,390
its hundred sections open
with almost these words.

79
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:21,298
So a tracker keeps a probability
on every word of every du'a,

80
00:05:21,298 --> 00:05:25,952
the way a map keeps your GPS dot
on roads you could have reached.

81
00:05:25,952 --> 00:05:31,177
Each update moves them on at the reader's
pace, then weighs them by the matches.

82
00:05:31,177 --> 00:05:37,365
With the next window only Kumayl fits, and
once a du'a holds 70%, its name is shown.

83
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:48,617
That's Shazam's idea, matches that line up
in time, applied to words as Tarteel does:

84
00:05:48,617 --> 00:05:52,262
it needs no copy of the
recording, only the text.

85
00:05:52,267 --> 00:05:57,832
On a test bench of voices held out of
training, read every way people read,

86
00:05:57,832 --> 00:06:03,249
the phone is on the right line 91% of
the time and on the right word 76%,

87
00:06:03,249 --> 00:06:06,885
and names the du'a within
10 seconds of its first

88
00:06:06,885 --> 00:06:10,676
words no other text shares 94% of the time.

89
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:19,288
It's weakest far from the phone, in a big
hall, and with du'as it doesn't have.

90
00:06:19,288 --> 00:06:24,112
For halls, we built a masjid in software:
a PA, loudspeakers in measured rooms,

91
00:06:24,112 --> 00:06:25,528
people talking.

92
00:06:25,528 --> 00:06:29,291
The phone went from 55%
to 67% on the line there,

93
00:06:29,291 --> 00:06:35,114
most of it from training in simulated
halls; the server reaches 81%.

94
00:06:35,750 --> 00:06:38,940
Whisper's text comes late
and has no timing inside it,

95
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:42,098
so the highlight comes
from the letter model.

96
00:06:42,098 --> 00:06:47,363
A decoder keeps a probability on every letter
of the du'a: the tracker's idea again,

97
00:06:47,363 --> 00:06:49,029
at a finer scale.

98
00:06:49,133 --> 00:06:50,824
Reading on is free.

99
00:06:50,824 --> 00:06:55,051
Starting the line again costs a
little, going back a few lines more,

100
00:06:55,051 --> 00:06:59,590
skipping ahead more still, so it
takes clear letters to move far.

101
00:06:59,590 --> 00:07:02,125
Here, the reader goes back two lines.

102
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:13,407
A pause makes no letters, and while no
voice is heard, stray letters cost more,

103
00:07:13,407 --> 00:07:16,645
so the highlight stays
on the last word said.

104
00:07:16,645 --> 00:07:20,746
Talk and a salawat between lines
have places of their own to go,

105
00:07:20,746 --> 00:07:25,511
and it enters the next line only
once it hears that line's words.

106
00:07:25,517 --> 00:07:31,862
When someone goes back or skips, it
follows within 3 seconds 84% of the time;

107
00:07:31,862 --> 00:07:36,830
through pauses, talk and
salawat, it holds still 91%.

108
00:07:36,833 --> 00:07:41,253
All of it runs in the phone's browser,
so no audio leaves the phone.

109
00:07:41,253 --> 00:07:45,786
A letter-model step takes about
7 milliseconds in Chrome on a desktop,

110
00:07:45,786 --> 00:07:48,117
because it reads only the new audio;

111
00:07:48,117 --> 00:07:53,508
Whisper takes under two seconds a window
on a phone, so it runs every two.

112
00:07:54,150 --> 00:07:56,005
So, from the top.

113
00:07:56,005 --> 00:07:59,757
Every two seconds, Whisper
writes down the last six;

114
00:07:59,757 --> 00:08:04,087
the words are matched against
all 522 texts in milliseconds;

115
00:08:04,087 --> 00:08:08,916
the tracker names the du'a once
the matches agree over time.

116
00:08:08,916 --> 00:08:13,882
Fifty times a second, the letter model
hears letters, and the decoder places them:

117
00:08:13,882 --> 00:08:15,875
the line, and the word.

118
00:08:21,683 --> 00:08:24,569
It stands on other people's
work: the reciters,

119
00:08:24,569 --> 00:08:28,929
the sites that publish their recordings
and texts, and the open models,

120
00:08:28,929 --> 00:08:31,064
data and tools on screen.
